# Django testing

Narrows `python`'s `pytest` testing guidance (`python/rules/testing.mdc`) to
Django's own test tooling: the Django `TestCase`/test `Client`, fixtures and
factories for model data, and `pytest-django` where a project has adopted
it. Applies to Django test files; a non-Django test module in the same repo
still follows `python`'s own testing rule.

## Choosing the test base

- Use `django.test.TestCase` for anything that touches the ORM — it wraps
  each test in a transaction that rolls back afterward, so tests stay
  isolated without manually cleaning up rows.
- Use `django.test.SimpleTestCase` (or mark it `databases = []`
  under `pytest-django`) for a test that touches no database at all — it
  skips the per-test transaction wrapper and is faster for pure logic.
- Where the project has adopted `pytest-django`, use its `@pytest.mark.django_db`
  fixture-based style (plain `def test_x(db, client): ...` functions,
  `django_user_model`, `rf` for `RequestFactory`) rather than mixing it with
  `unittest`-style `TestCase` subclasses in the same suite — match whichever
  the project already uses; do not introduce the other style alongside it.

## The test client

- Drive request/response behavior through `self.client`
  (`TestCase`)/the `client` fixture (`pytest-django`), not by calling a view
  function directly with a hand-built request object — the client exercises
  URL resolution, middleware, and template rendering the same way a real
  request does.
- Assert on `response.status_code`, `response.context`, and
  `response.templates` (or the parsed JSON body for an API) rather than
  inspecting internal view state that the client's response object doesn't
  expose.
- Log a test user in through `self.client.force_login(user)` (fast, skips
  the auth backend) or `self.client.login(...)` (exercises the real auth
  backend) — pick `force_login` for tests where the auth mechanism itself
  isn't what's under test.

## Model data: fixtures and factories

- Prefer a factory (`factory_boy`'s `DjangoModelFactory`, if the project
  already depends on it) over a JSON/YAML fixture file for test data —
  factories build valid model instances from code, so a new required field
  doesn't silently break every fixture file that predates it.
- Keep a `conftest.py`/factory module for related test data in the
  narrowest app-level location that covers the tests needing it, matching
  `python`'s own "narrowest `conftest.py`" guidance.
- Build only the fields a test actually asserts on or that a model
  constraint requires; a factory that sets every field to a fixed literal
  makes tests brittle to unrelated schema changes.

## Forms, views, and permissions

- Test a `Form`/DRF `Serializer` by constructing it directly with test data
  and asserting `is_valid()`/`.errors`, separately from a full
  client-driven view test — this isolates a validation bug from a
  view-wiring bug.
- For a permission-gated view, assert both the authorized case (200/302 to
  the expected page) and the unauthorized case (302 to login, or 403) —
  a view test that only exercises the happy path never proves the
  permission check actually runs.

## Migrations in tests

- Do not disable migrations for the whole suite (`MIGRATION_MODULES` set to
  a no-op) purely for speed unless the project has already made that
  tradeoff deliberately — a suite that skips migrations can pass while a
  real deploy's migration path is broken.
- A test asserting data-migration behavior (a `RunPython` step) belongs in
  its own test using `django.test.migrations`' testing utilities or the
  project's configured migration-test helper, not folded into an unrelated
  model test.

## Determinism

- Freeze time (`freezegun`, or `pytest-django`'s equivalent) instead of
  asserting against `timezone.now()` computed twice in the test and the
  code under test — a real few-millisecond drift between the two calls is a
  known source of flaky Django tests.
- Never depend on a specific queryset row order unless the code under test
  applies an explicit `order_by()` — Django does not guarantee row order
  without one, even for a small SQLite test database.
